C='Positive integersX,Y; proper divisor ofY is positive divisor other thanY.'
code='def proper_factor(x,y):\n    return y % x == 0 and x != y'
e(14,'a',9,2,'Construct','Construct a logic expression that evaluates to True if X is a proper divisor of Y and to False otherwise.',C,['B2.3.2'],'B',15,'Remainder zero AND unequal operands.','For positive integers a and b,construct a Python Boolean expression that is True exactly when a divides b with no remainder and a is smaller than b.',pts((1,'b % a ==0.'),(1,'Combined using and with a < b.')),solution='b % a == 0 and a < b',reason='Preserves arithmetic/relational conjunction reasoning using Python; positive domain removes divide-by-zero ambiguity.')
code='def proper_sum(n):\n    total=0\n    for factor in range(1,n):\n        if n % factor == 0:\n            total += factor\n    return total'
e(14,'b',9,3,'Construct','Construct an algorithm in pseudocode for the subprogram sumPD(N).','N>1; sum proper divisors; sumPD16=15.',['B2.3.2','B2.3.3','B2.3.4'],'B',15,'Initialize sum,loop eligible divisors,add when remainder0; old sqrt-bound alternative without paired factor is a sourceMS weakness.','Construct proper_sum(n) in Python to return the sum of positive divisors smaller than n. Assume n is a positive integer; proper_sum(1) must return0.',pts((1,'Initializes total to0 and returns it after processing.'),(1,'Loops over all possible proper divisors1..n-1 (or a valid optimized equivalent).'),(1,'Adds a candidate only when n % candidate ==0.')),solution=code,code=code,tests='assert [proper_sum(n) for n in [1,6,9,12,28]]==[0,6,4,16,28]',reason='Preserves conditional accumulation; Python conversion explicitly includes n=1. Do not use sourceMS sqrt-bound suggestion without correctly adding paired divisors.')
code='def proper_sum(n):\n    total=0\n    for factor in range(1,n):\n        if n % factor == 0:\n            total += factor\n    return total\n\ndef classify_grid(grid):\n    low,equal,high=[],[],[]\n    for row in grid:\n        for n in row:\n            summed=proper_sum(n)\n            if summed < n:\n                low.append(n)\n            elif summed == n:\n                equal.append(n)\n            else:\n                high.append(n)\n    return low,equal,high'
e(14,'c',[9,10],10,'Construct','Construct an algorithm in pseudocode to create the three one-dimensional arrays,ABUNDANT,DEFICIENT and PERFECT,as described. You may assume MAT is inputted and three arrays of sufficient size initialized. You should call sumPD() in your algorithm.','Grid4×6 [6,12,20,42,3,48],[78,28,70,80,2,16],[60,72,10,56,9,54],[30,13,7,11,15,17]. Classify by proper-divisor sum relative to original.',['B2.2.2','B2.3.2','B2.3.3','B2.3.4'],'B',[16,17],'Traverse both dimensions;call sumPD;compare to input;append each value to correct one-dimensional output;more than10 alternative criteria capped.','The function proper_sum(n) returns the sum of proper divisors of positive integer n. Construct classify_grid(grid) in Python. Return three lists(low,equal,high) containing each grid value whose proper-divisor sum is respectively less than,equal to,or greater than that value. Preserve row-major order and all repeated values. Do not modify grid; call proper_sum for each value.',pts((1,'Initializes three distinct empty output lists.'),(1,'Traverses each input row.'),(1,'Traverses every value in the row.'),(1,'Calls proper_sum on that value.'),(1,'Uses comparison with the original value,not an index.'),(1,'Appends to low if summed<n.'),(1,'Appends to equal if summed==n.'),(1,'Appends to high if summed>n.'),(1,'Preserves row-major order and duplicates,with each value assigned exactly once.'),(1,'Returns the three lists in the required order after traversal.')),solution=code,code=code,tests='assert classify_grid([[1,6,12],[9,28,12]])==([1,9],[6,28],[12,12])\nassert classify_grid([])==([],[],[])',assets=[pageasset('SRC-005',9,'Definitions'),pageasset('SRC-005',10,'Original full matrix and output arrays')],reason='Preserves nested traversal,function decomposition and three-way classification; Python dynamic outputs remove legacy fixed-array index bookkeeping while retaining10 substantive marking points.')
